<hr>

<div>
![](../../src/images/hex_complex.png){.intro_image fig-alt="Course logo"}

Collar studies, protected-area networks, and monitoring programs often require the same summary for each animal, reserve, or site: a map, a table, and a paragraph of interpretation.

We previously built a Quarto document that can be regenerated from its data (see ***4.6 Communicating spatial data with Quarto***). A parameterized document also accepts arguments, so the same source file can produce a separate report for each site while keeping the analysis, tables, maps, and citations together.

In completing this lesson, you will learn how to:

* Declare parameters in a Quarto document's header and use them in its code
* Render one report for each member of a set with `quarto_render()` and *purrr*
* Put the numbers behind a map into a cross-referenced table
* Cite the source of every layer a map is built from

**Important!** Before starting this tutorial, be sure that you have completed all preliminary and previous lessons!

*Note: This lesson is optional. Nothing in the problem sets requires it. A report that can be regenerated allows collaborators and reviewers to check the analysis behind its results.*

</div>

